2403 Q 2547 2445 2732 2521 Q 2918 2598 3046 2688 Q 3123 2739 3148 2793 Q 3174 2848 3174 2963 Q 3174 3187 3104 3430 Q 3034 3674 2890 3885 Q 2746 4096 2525 4227 Q 2304 4358 2003 4358 Q 1709 4358 1472 4224 Q 1235 4090 1097 3856 Q 960 3622 960 3328 Q 960 3059 1085 2838 Q 1210 2618 1434 2490 Q 1658 2362 1965 2362 L 1965 2362 z\n\" transform=\"scale(0.015625)\"/></defs><use xlink:href=\"#LibreFranklin-Regular-89\"/><use xlink:href=\"#LibreFranklin-Regular-ee\" transform=\"translate(65.796875 0)\"/></g></g><g id=\"text_18\"><g style=\"fill: #333333\" transform=\"translate(178.356682 93.448552) scale(0.08 -0.08)\"><use xlink:href=\"#LibreFranklin-Regular-83\"/><use xlink:href=\"#LibreFranklin-Regular-ee\" transform=\"translate(64.09375 0)\"/></g></g><g id=\"text_19\"><g style=\"fill: #333333\" transform=\"translate(154.062804 116.041655) scale(0.08 -0.08)\"><use xlink:href=\"#LibreFranklin-Regular-82\"/><use xlink:href=\"#LibreFranklin-Regular-ee\" transform=\"translate(59.90625 0)\"/></g></g><g id=\"text_20\"><g style=\"fill: #333333\" transform=\"translate(154.062804 138.634759) scale(0.08 -0.08)\"><use xlink:href=\"#LibreFranklin-Regular-82\"/><use xlink:href=\"#LibreFranklin-Regular-ee\" transform=\"translate(59.90625 0)\"/></g></g></g></g><defs><clipPath id=\"pfd11c2afbf\"><rect x=\"102.073906\" y=\"7.2\" width=\"357.12\" height=\"144.144\"/></clipPath></defs></svg>\n","title":"Exhibit 1 · Sales lost by category at supermarkets near a discounter (other regions)","note":null},"answer":"<p>Losses concentrate in <b>packaged groceries and household goods</b> (13-14%), where products are identical and price wins. Fresh categories barely move (2-3%). <b>So-what:</b> fight on price only for packaged staples, and lean into fresh, where FreshMart is stronger.</p>"},{"q":"Option A: match the discounter's prices on 300 key packaged items in the 30 exposed stores. That gives up 3 margin points on 20% of their sales, and halves the sustained sales loss (8% → 4%). Worth it?","exhibit":null,"answer":"<p>Cost = 30 × $20M × 20% × 3% = <b>$3.6M/year</b>. Benefit = sales loss falls by 4% → 30 × $20M × 4% × 25% = <b>$6.0M/year</b> saved.</p> <p>Net <b>+$2.4M</b> vs doing nothing. Remaining damage ≈ −12 + 6 − 3.6 = <b>−$9.6M</b>. Price matching helps, but it isn't enough on its own.</p>"},{"q":"What else should FreshMart do?","exhibit":null,"answer":"<ul><li><b>Private label</b> on packaged staples: discounter-level prices at better margins</li> <li><b>Double down on fresh</b>: bigger produce and deli counters in exposed stores; the discounter's range is thin here</li> <li><b>Loyalty programme</b> with personalised offers to keep high-value families</li> <li><b>Don't</b> cut prices chain-wide: 30 unaffected stores would give away margin for nothing</li></ul>"}],"reco":"Doing nothing puts $12M a year at risk, about a quarter of FreshMart's profit. Respond in the 30 exposed stores only: match prices on ~300 packaged staples (net +$2.4M), launch a private-label range in the same categories, and invest in fresh food, where the discounter is weak and customers don't leave. Avoid a chain-wide price war. Next step: pilot the full package in the first 5 stores the discounter targets, and track weekly sales by category.","great":["Compares every option against doing nothing","Uses the category chart to decide where to fight","Keeps the response limited to the stores that are actually exposed"],"id":7,"slug":"freshmart"}